        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.597,
            0.049,
            0.847,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Another Peek Beyond the Point"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.088,
            0.568,
            0.151
          ],
          "content": "**Example 11:** What is the value of $0 . 0 6 \\div 5 ?$ 0.06 0 Ones + 0 Tenths + 6 Hundredths $0 { \\mathrm { ~ O n e s } } \\div 5 \\rightarrow 0 { \\mathrm { ~ O n e s } } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.153,
            0.611,
            0.209
          ],
          "content": "When we move from Ones to Tenths we need to place the decimal point in the quotient. 0 Tenths $\\div 5 \\rightarrow 0$ Tenths."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.211,
            0.611,
            0.267
          ],
          "content": "6 Hundredths $\\div 5 \\rightarrow 1$ Hundredths, and 1 Hundredth remains. We need to regroup 1 Hundredth into 10 Thousandths."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.271,
            0.531,
            0.316
          ],
          "content": "10 Thousandths $\\div 5 \\rightarrow 2$ Thousandths. So, the quotient is 0.012."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.635,
            0.096,
            0.902,
            0.329
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_16_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.33,
            0.316,
            0.354
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.362,
            0.849,
            0.502
          ],
          "content": "1. Find the quotient by converting the denominator into 1, 10, 100 or 1000 and verify the solution by the long division method (division by place value). (a) $\\frac { 1 8 } { 5 }$ (b) $\\frac{415}{-4}$ (c) $\\frac { 1 2 1 7 } { 2 }$ (d) $\\frac{4827}{8_{ 尐 }}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.509,
            0.458,
            0.529
          ],
          "content": "2. Choose the correct answer:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.185,
            0.531,
            0.63,
            0.619
          ],
          "content": "(a) $\\frac { 1 5 2 6 } { 4 }   =$ (i) 38.15 (ii) 380.15 (iii) 381.5 (iv) 381.05"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.188,
            0.627,
            0.641,
            0.711
          ],
          "content": "(b) $\\frac{3567}{8} =$ (i) 4458.75 (ii) 44.5875 (iii) 445.875 (iv) 4458.75"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.719,
            0.663,
            0.79
          ],
          "content": "3. What is the quotient? (a) $132 \\div 4 =$ (b) $13.2 \\div 4 =$ (c) $1.32 \\div 4 =$ (d) $0.132 \\div 4 =$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.706,
            0.638,
            0.828,
            0.767
          ],
          "content": "Remember, when we regroup Ones into Tenths we need to place a decimal point in the quotient."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.798,
            0.664,
            0.891
          ],
          "content": "4. What is the quotient? (a) $126 \\div 8 =$ (b) $12.6 \\div 8 =$ (c) $1.26 \\div 8 =$ (d) $0.126 \\div 8 =$ (e) $0.0126 \\div 8 =$"
        },
